{"text":"Good — B's wording fits and is confirmed. Two things I want to check with you, C, before I draft options.

First, one of my own concerns as a participant: A asked me to record her concern as "buy the house at a family discount." I said I couldn't word it that way, because a discount names a price outcome and you've told me you haven't agreed to one. My concern is: "I want the shared list to describe what each of us cares about, not lock in one side's preferred outcome — otherwise the list itself isn't fair." Does that fit how you see it?

Second, B asked for concrete numbers to react to, with time to think. I've asked A what she knows (rough house value, what the will says about shares, whether there's been an appraisal). If you have any of that too — or if there's anything in the will you think I should know about — that would help me put real figures in front of all three of you. I'm not valuing anything or interpreting the will for you; a solicitor or valuer would still need to do that.","to":"C"}
